//! The Auk runtime, in Rust.
//!
//! Every export is a C-ABI function (or static) with a fixed symbol name: code generated by the
//! LLVM back end calls them (the JIT resolves them in the compiler's own process, `auk build`
//! links `libauk_rt.a`). The `#[repr(C)]` types below are the layouts generated code relies on
//! (`src/mir/layout.rs` computes the same layouts; `src/mir/llvm/intrin.rs` inlines accesses to
//! `AukHeader` and `AukArr` fields by offset).
//!
//! # Ownership conventions
//! 1. Heap objects (AukStr, AukArr, AukMap, AukErr, AukObj/AukEnv,
//!    AukTask, AukConn, AukListener) start with a AukHeader and are reference counted.
//!    A freshly created object has rc == 1.
//! 2. Unless a comment says otherwise, function ARGUMENTS ARE BORROWED (the
//!    callee retains anything it keeps) and RETURNED OBJECTS ARE OWNED (+1;
//!    the caller must release them).  Results inside AukOpt_* / AukRes_*
//!    are owned too (both `val` and `err`).
//! 3. Elements passed *into* a container (push/set/insert, map set, Value
//!    constructors) are borrowed: the container retains its own copy using
//!    the element ops.  Elements written to a `void *out` parameter are owned
//!    by the caller.  `auk_arr_at` / `auk_map_at` / `auk_map_find` return
//!    a BORROWED pointer into the container's storage (valid until the
//!    container is mutated or released).
//! 4. Functions that mutate a container take `AukArr **` / `AukMap **` (a
//!    Auk `var`).  They copy first when the object is shared (rc > 1) or is
//!    a slice view (copy-on-write), so other holders never see the change.
//! 5. NULL is accepted everywhere a AukStr* is read and means "".  retain and
//!    release accept NULL.  NULL is also accepted for empty AukArr* and AukMap*
//!    in read-only functions, but codegen should prefer real empty objects
//!    (an array needs its element ops).
//! 6. `loc` parameters are "file:line:col" strings used in panic messages.
//!
//! Panics print `panic: <msg>\n  at <loc>` to stderr and exit with 101, unless the thread has a
//! recovery point (`catch`, a task thread: see catch.rs).
//!
//! # Layout of the crate
//! One module per area; public entry points are `auk_*` (`#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe
//! extern "C"`), internal helpers `auk__*` (plain `pub unsafe fn`, or `extern "C"` when used
//! as a callback). Cross-module calls use the path `crate::module::name`.
#![allow(non_camel_case_types, non_snake_case, non_upper_case_globals, clippy::missing_safety_doc)]
// Every export is a C-ABI entry point that takes raw pointers from generated code by design;
// `as` casts on libc types (time_t, ...) are kept for portability across platforms.
#![allow(clippy::not_unsafe_ptr_arg_deref, clippy::unnecessary_cast)]

